We are given that Jon buys twice as many red ties as blue ties. Let $r$ be the number of red ties and $b$ be the number of blue ties. Then $r = 2b$.
We are also given that red ties cost 50% more than blue ties. So $R = B + 0.5B = 1.5B$.
We are given that Jon spends $200 on blue ties and each blue tie costs $40. So $b \times 40 = 200$. Therefore, $b = \frac{200}{40} = 5$.
Since $r = 2b$, we have $r = 2 \times 5 = 10$.
The cost of each red tie is $R = 1.5B = 1.5 \times 40 = 60$.
The total cost of red ties is $r \times R = 10 \times 60 = 600$.
The total cost of all ties is the cost of blue ties plus the cost of red ties, which is $200 + 600 = 800$.
The total number of ties is $r+b = 10+5 = 15$.
The cost per tie is $\frac{800}{15} = \frac{160}{3} \approx 53.33$.

